# 0029 - an unconfirmed peer gets thirty seconds, not twelve.
#
# S1-AA's join-phase shape, read to the line with patch 0028 in
# runs/split140414-9 (2026-09-10). The far seat -- the relay-only one --
# handshaked with the founder at 76.1 s and at 76.7 sent handshake request
# attempt 1 to the seven other seats BY UDP, to the LAN addresses the
# founder's peer list carries, which its network cannot route. Attempt 2
# at 79.0 went by TCP (try_tcp_fallback alternates: attempts % 2 == 1),
# attempt 3 by UDP, attempt 4 by TCP at 85.0. Four seats answered the TCP
# attempt within three seconds. Three (n2, n5, n7) never saw any of the
# four: their own first attempts toward the far seat were "carried by
# nothing" at 79.9-85.4 s -- they were not yet on a relay the far seat
# could reach them through, so its out-of-band requests had nowhere to
# land -- and the twelve-second reaper took the three entries at 88.0 s.
# Their own TCP requests then arrived, the far seat answered by UDP to the
# request's source address (which routes), and they printed "handshaked
# with" at 85.2, 87.6 and 90.6 -- on entries the far seat had just
# deleted. It re-added them at 88.8-90.3 and read 7 seen/5 confirmed/8
# wanted at 91.1 s. Every attempt was carried at both ends and no
# receive-side drop line printed anywhere: the packets die between the
# ends, half of them by design.
#
# So the twelve-second budget holds exactly two TCP attempts, the first
# of which lands only if the target already sits on a shared relay. This
# patch makes it thirty seconds: five TCP attempts. A dead entry lingers
# eighteen seconds longer, and nothing depends on an unconfirmed entry
# going away quickly. Local behaviour; nothing on the wire changes; the
# constant is also the base of LOAD_PEERS_TIMEOUT, which moves from 22 to
# 40 s and is the interval at which an empty peer list is re-seeded from
# the saved one -- a wait that a group with peers never takes.
#
# THE PREDICTION IS BOOKED BEFORE THE RUN, a plain 8+1 run: the far seat
# reaps NOTHING before 100 s and reads 8/8 confirmed within twenty seconds
# of its founder handshake. The falsifier is a reap with "attempts 8-10,
# last packet 30 s ago" -- the same shape, only later -- which would mean
# the target's relay path is the whole story and the timeout was never the
# lever. Fix (a) is named and not built: send TCP on every attempt when
# the peer has never been heard directly, so the first attempt is not a
# UDP packet to a LAN address on another network.
#
# THE FIRST RUN (runs/split141942-9): the first clause held, the second
# could not be tested. The far seat reaped nothing in 240 s -- against 6,
# 4, 6 and 3 reaps in four of the six runs before the patch -- and
# handshaked with every seat that was there within seconds of its
# arrival: the founder at 32.8 s, two seats five seconds after its
# requests, each later arrival on the TCP response attempt two to three
# seconds after the UDP one failed. It read 7/7/7 at 91.8 s and 8/8/8 at
# 181.8, because the ninth seat entered the group only at 160.7 s: its
# own line at 150 s says "Tox is offline; of 20 known nodes, 20
# bootstrapped and 20 TCP relays were accepted" -- a node whose Tox had
# no DHT for 150 s, so no friend connection and no invite. A bootstrap
# wait the client already names, not the handshake cycle. The reap census
# with the patch stands at 0 of 1; one run is one run, and every 8+1 run
# from here reads itself.

diff --git a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.h b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.h
index c9a0f84..a481216 100644
--- a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.h
+++ b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_chats.h
@@ -32,7 +32,23 @@ extern "C" {
 #define GC_PING_TIMEOUT 12
 #define GC_SEND_IP_PORT_INTERVAL (GC_PING_TIMEOUT * 5)
 #define GC_CONFIRMED_PEER_TIMEOUT (GC_PING_TIMEOUT * 4 + 10)
-#define GC_UNCONFIRMED_PEER_TIMEOUT GC_PING_TIMEOUT
+/* p2p-poker (patch 0029): an unconfirmed peer gets thirty seconds, not twelve.
+ *
+ * Upstream reaps a peer that has not confirmed twelve seconds after its
+ * last packet -- GC_PING_TIMEOUT -- and a handshake attempt goes out every
+ * GC_SEND_HANDSHAKE_INTERVAL = 3 s, alternating UDP and TCP, so the budget
+ * holds four attempts and only two of them by TCP. For a peer on another
+ * network that is the whole budget: its UDP attempts go to the LAN
+ * addresses the peer list carries, which its network cannot route, and
+ * its TCP attempts land only once the target sits on a relay it shares.
+ * Read to the line on 2026-09-10 (S1-AA, runs/split140414-9): the far
+ * seat handshaked with four of seven seats on its TCP attempt at +2.3 s,
+ * reaped the other three at +12 s while their own requests were still
+ * on the way, then answered those requests on entries it had just
+ * deleted, and started over. Thirty seconds holds five TCP attempts. A
+ * dead entry lingers eighteen seconds longer, which nothing depends on.
+ * Local behaviour: nothing on the wire changes. */
+#define GC_UNCONFIRMED_PEER_TIMEOUT 30
 
 #define GC_JOIN_DATA_LENGTH (ENC_PUBLIC_KEY_SIZE + CHAT_ID_SIZE)
 
